  "markdown_report": "At 0% progress, prioritize **recency-balanced terminal-mask training** within the corrected BERT4Rec baseline. All proposed gains remain hypotheses.\n\n**Insights and directions.** Starting-point evidence gives three insights: (1) Industrial and Electronics have 12.25% and 11.27% negative-over-positive rates. Their negative lower-tail margins motivate better discrimination, rather than collapse remedies. (2) Users report stale priorities and broadly plausible recommendations. Training should emphasize specific next choices, although histories cannot establish equipment compatibility. (3) Collapse scores near 0.5 imply approximately zero mean cosine similarity. Empty category breakdowns cannot support category-specific explanations.\n\nRelated work supplies three insights: (1) BERT4Rec already includes terminal masking to align training and inference [1]. Increasing its exposure is an objective-allocation experiment, not a new backbone. (2) Recency Sampling motivates emphasizing recent targets [2]. Transfer to these four datasets requires validation. (3) Adaptive hardness addresses difficult competitors [3]. Its collaborative-filtering evidence does not establish benefits for masked sequential prediction.\n\nThese observations organize three directions: inference-aligned supervision, discriminative sampling, and richer item representations.\n\n| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed experiment | Remaining gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Supervision | BERT4Rec terminal masking | Recent-prefix curriculum | Immediate intent is unobserved |\n| Sampling | Adaptive hardness | Warmed-up semi-hard negatives | False negatives |\n| Representation | Joint history/content alignment | Scratch-trained title encoder | Sparse titles; no pretrained weights |\n\n**Candidate assessment.** Scores are originality/future potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder.\n\n1. **Terminal-prefix curriculum: 3/7/7/7/2.** Originality: focused adaptation (+), established terminal masking (−). Potential: inexpensive transferable objective (+), gains may saturate (−). User: targets recent priorities and specific next choices (+), cannot verify compatibility (−). Model: targets weak separation at inference positions (+), measured margins are not validation HR (−). Difficulty: training-only change (+), masking audit required (−).\n2. **Conservative semi-hard sampling: 4/7/5/8/4.** Originality: sequential adaptation (+), familiar mining (−). Potential: informative competitors (+), sampling bias (−). User: distinguishes close alternatives (+), intent remains implicit (−). Model: targets negative lower-tail margins (+), false negatives (−). Difficulty: unchanged architecture (+), exclusions and mining cost (−).\n3. **Joint scratch title encoder: 4/6/7/5/6.** Originality: task-specific integration (+), established content features (−). Potential: finer identity cues (+), sparse text (−). User: specific artist/function clues (+), compatibility remains uncertain (−). Model: potentially sharper representations (+), collapse is absent (−). Difficulty: one joint network (+), predictor/schema changes (−).\n\n**Selected algorithm.** Mix reconstruction with terminal prediction on training-only prefixes; ramp terminal weight from 0.25 to 0.75. Sample terminal cutoffs equally from all eligible positions and the final history quartile. This targets stale-interest ranking and broad-versus-specific next-choice errors, while testing whether weak terminal discrimination improves.\n\n```python\nt = sample_training_cutoff(history, recent_mix=0.5)\nx = left_pad(history[:t] + [MASK])\nL_terminal = CE(model(x)[-1], history[t])\nloss = (1-w)*mean(L_reconstruction) + w*L_terminal\n```\n\nNormalize objectives separately. Keep one checkpoint/category, fixed seeds, ≤200 epochs and 200 batches/epoch. Preserve routing, diagnostics, and appended-mask prediction; select solely by fixed macro validation HR@10.\n\nReferences: [1] https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690; [2] https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643; [3] https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/28709.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Recency-balanced terminal-mask curriculum for the supplied BERT4Rec: jointly optimize ordinary masked reconstruction and next-item prediction from strictly truncated training prefixes, increasing terminal supervision during training.",
    "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies stale-interest recommendations and broad matches outranking more specific next choices. Terminal supervision emphasizes choosing the actual next interaction from its available context. Industrial_and_Scientific and Electronics also show negative tenth-percentile margins, motivating a test of improved terminal discrimination. Compatibility and immediate intent remain unobserved; improvement is not established.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain the supplied bidirectional Transformer and reserved mask token. For a zero-based target index t >= 1, terminal input contains history[:t] followed by MASK, with no target or subsequent events. Cap context at L-1 items before appending MASK and left-pad to L. Compute reconstruction loss only at masked positions whose original identities are hidden. Average each objective over its own valid targets before mixing. Use an explicit proposed linear terminal-weight schedule from 0.25 to 0.75 over the configured training duration; sample cutoffs from a 50:50 mixture of all eligible training positions and their final quartile. These are initial hyperparameters, not empirical optima. Preserve preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs, 200 batches per epoch, all four categories, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and trial-local artifacts. Each category loads exactly one selected checkpoint. Keep predictor writer and training architecture synchronized. Preserve ModelView interfaces and DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Diagnostic margins must use features at actual masked target positions, with original target IDs retained only as labels; never diagnose using visible targets. Read appended-mask features at the final nonpadding position, never count-1. Do not use unavailable metadata categories or treat unidentified-item skips as negative labels. Select using the pinned validation macro HR@10; report margins only as secondary diagnostics.",
    "pseudocode": "for category in four_categories:\n    initialize supplied BERT4Rec with seed 42\n    for epoch in range(configured_epochs_at_most_200):\n        w = linear_schedule(epoch, start=0.25, end=0.75)\n        for step in range(200):\n            histories = sample_training_histories()\n            reconstruction_input, reconstruction_labels = baseline_masked_batch(histories)\n            reconstruction_loss = mean_CE_at_supervised_mask_positions(reconstruction_input, reconstruction_labels)\n            terminal_inputs, terminal_labels = [], []\n            for history in histories:\n                t = sample_cutoff(history, mixture_of_uniform_and_recent_quartile=0.5)\n                context = history[max(0, t-(L-1)):t]\n                terminal_inputs.append(left_pad(context + [MASK], L))\n                terminal_labels.append(history[t])\n            features = model.log2feats(terminal_inputs)\n            terminal_features = gather_final_nonpadding(features, terminal_inputs)\n            terminal_loss = mean_CE(model.predict_score(terminal_features, catalog_items), terminal_labels)\n            optimize((1-w)*reconstruction_loss + w*terminal_loss)\n    retain_one_validation_selected_checkpoint(category)\nwrite_predictor_with_identical_architecture_and_appended_mask_inference()",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "Combines recency-balanced prefix selection with explicit allocation of terminal and reconstruction supervision in this constrained benchmark.",
      "negative": "Terminal masking is already part of original BERT4Rec; the contribution is an experimental training policy."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "A small, interpretable change can establish whether inference-aligned supervision merits further study across categories.",
      "negative": "Interaction order is an imperfect proxy for immediate preference, and benefits may depend on history length."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 2,
      "positive": "Preserves the architecture and scoring function; changes mainly concern training-example construction and loss weighting.",
      "negative": "Prefix boundaries, separate loss normalization, and diagnostic masking require careful consistency checks."
    }
  },
